  ],
  "mechanisms": [
    "Thermal adaptation: people take spontaneous behavioural actions (e.g., adjusting clothing) and psychological adjustments (e.g., differentiation of seasonal comfort expectations) to maintain thermal comfort across seasons.",
    "Semantic artifact hypothesis: people may describe their preferred thermal state with different adjectives in different seasons (e.g., 'slightly cool' in summer vs. 'slightly warm' in winter), leading to discrepancies between neutral and preferred temperatures.",
    "Previous experience: subjects' preferences for wind and humidity are influenced by their past experiences of how these factors help eliminate discomfort."
  ],
  "moderators": [
    "Season (hot vs. cool)",
    "Air temperature (Ta)",
    "Mean radiant temperature (Tmrt)",
    "Vapour pressure (VP)",
    "Air speed (v)",
    "Standard Effective Temperature (SET*)"
  ],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "The results can contribute to the planning and design of outdoor environments in hot-humid regions to support the use of outdoor spaces and increase occupant satisfaction.",
    "A reasonable hot season design temperature range for outdoor recreational and sporting facilities in central Taiwan is about 29\u00b0C operative temperature.",
    "An acceptable temperature range for Taiwan's cool season is 23.7-29.7\u00b0C.",
    "If meteorological observations exceed acceptable ranges in the hot season, more shade should be provided to reduce Tmrt.",
    "Elevated air speeds may be effective in bringing unacceptably high temperatures back into the acceptable range of 26-31\u00b0C in summer."
  ],
  "domains": [
    "A7_Haptic_Thermal",
    "A10_Temporal",
